  │ [38;5;153mui[39m            │ Preact components: task list, task editor, bell + feed dropdown, activity view  │ all of the    │
  │               │                                                                                 │ above         │
  └───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────┘

  The load-bearing decision: [1mthe scheduler does not write notifications directly.[0m It reports crossings; [38;5;153mnotifications[39m
  decides what becomes a feed entry. That keeps "when did this cross its due date" separate from "have I already told
  you about it," which is exactly the seam that gets tangled otherwise — and it's what makes the fired-set testable
  without waiting on real clock time.

  The scheduler takes [38;5;153mnow[39m as a parameter rather than calling [38;5;153mDate.now()[39m internally. Time-based features are untestable
  otherwise, and this one is nearly all time.

  [1mWhy this survives a server later:[0m [38;5;153mstore[39m and [38;5;153mlog[39m are the only things that touch persistence, and the log is already an
  event stream. Swapping [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m for a sync adapter is a change in two modules, not five.

  Alongside the architecture, the tooling question — cheapest to answer now, before any code exists. Whatever you pick
  lands in the spec's Global Constraints so every later task inherits it.
